@*
    One mark for "this was proved", used everywhere a fact about an account is proved rather than
    merely asserted (item 237).

    WHY IT IS SHARED. Four screens were inventing four of these: a green "Confirmed" pill on the
    emails card, a bare tick with a date in the admin grid, an "On" pill on the two-factor panel,
    and prose in the profile hero. Four marks that mean the same thing and look like four different
    things teach a reader that a tick means whatever this screen felt like — and the day one of them
    is wrong, nobody notices, because there is nothing to compare it against.

    THE RULE IT ENFORCES, and the reason it is a component rather than a CSS class: an unproved
    fact renders NOTHING unless there is something a person can do about it. `Unproved` is the
    sentence naming that something, and leaving it null is how a caller says "there is no path".
    Drawing "Not verified" against a fact nobody can change is a dead end — the same failure as a
    refusal with no way through — and this makes it impossible to ship by accident rather than
    something to remember. It is why phone numbers get no mark here: `UserPhone.IsValidated`
    exists, and nothing in the product can ever set it.

    Colour carries one meaning: proved. Nothing else on this component is ever green.
*@

@if (Proved)
{
    @if (Chip)
    {
        <span class="badge rounded-pill bg-success-subtle text-success-emphasis @Class"
              title="@ProvedTitle" @attributes="Extra">
            <BenIcon Name="check" Class="me-1" />@Label
        </span>
    }
    else
    {
        <span class="text-success @Class" title="@ProvedTitle" @attributes="Extra">
            <BenIcon Name="check" Title="@ProvedTitle" />
            @if (ShowLabel)
            {
                <span class="small ms-1">@Label</span>
            }
        </span>
    }
}
else if (!string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(Unproved))
{
    @if (Chip)
    {
        <span class="badge rounded-pill bg-warning-subtle text-warning-emphasis @Class"
              title="@Unproved" @attributes="Extra">
            <BenIcon Name="alert-circle" Class="me-1" />@UnprovedLabel
        </span>
    }
    else
    {
        <span class="text-warning @Class" title="@Unproved" @attributes="Extra">
            <BenIcon Name="alert-circle" Title="@Unproved" />
            @if (ShowLabel)
            {
                <span class="small ms-1">@UnprovedLabel</span>
            }
        </span>
    }
}

@code {
    /// <summary>What was proved, in the words a reader wants: "Confirmed", "Two-factor on".</summary>
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public string Label { get; set; } = "Confirmed";

    /// <summary>Whether it is proved.</summary>
    [Parameter] public bool Proved { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// What to do about it when it is not proved yet — shown as the tooltip, and its presence is
    /// what allows the unproved state to be drawn at all.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// Null means there is no path, and then NOTHING is drawn. See the remarks at the top: a mark
    /// that can never turn is worse than no mark, because it reads as a fault the person caused.
    /// </remarks>
    [Parameter] public string? Unproved { get; set; }

    /// <summary>What the unproved state is called. Defaults to "Not " plus the label, lowercased.</summary>
    [Parameter] public string? UnprovedText { get; set; }

    /// <summary>When it was proved. Appended to the tooltip, in US format like the rest of the site.</summary>
    [Parameter] public DateTime? On { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Anything more the tooltip should say — how it was proved, by whom.</summary>
    [Parameter] public string? Detail { get; set; }

    /// <summary>A pill, for a row of chips. Otherwise a bare tick beside a value.</summary>
    [Parameter] public bool Chip { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Whether the bare form prints its label as well as its tick. Chips always do.</summary>
    [Parameter] public bool ShowLabel { get; set; } = true;

    [Parameter] public string? Class { get; set; }

    [Parameter(CaptureUnmatchedValues = true)]
    public IReadOnlyDictionary<string, object>? Extra { get; set; }

    private string UnprovedLabel =>
        UnprovedText ?? $"Not {Label.ToLowerInvariant()}";

    /// <summary>
    /// The tooltip on a proved fact: what, when, and anything else worth saying.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// The date is the point. "Confirmed" alone invites the question this answers, and an
    /// administrator looking at somebody else's account is usually asking exactly when.
    /// </remarks>
    private string ProvedTitle
    {
        get
        {
            var parts = new List<string> { Label };
            if (On is { } on) parts.Add(on.ToString("MM/dd/yyyy"));
            if (!string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(Detail)) parts.Add(Detail!);
            return string.Join(" · ", parts);
        }
    }
}
